s let's just give some  names to these points we'll call this s  1 and this point s 2 and the unit  vectors over here where R 1 and theta 1  when we're at the point as 2 we have to  choose unit vectors exactly the same way  our had two points in the direction of  increasing R and theta hat two points in  the direction of increasing theta so  what we see in polar coordinates is that  R hat one is not equal to R hat - why  are they not equal they both are unit  vectors so they both have the same  magnitude but they point in opposite  directions in the same way that theta  hat 1 is not equal to theta hat 2 so  unlike Cartesian coordinates in which at  every single point had the same unit  vectors in polar coordinates the unit  vectors depend on where you are in space  and that will make our analysis in polar  coordinates a little bit more  complicated
 

